# Line endings are decided HERE, not by each machine's core.autocrlf.
#
# The index is already 100% LF (293 of 293 tracked files at the time this landed),
# so this normalises nothing and changes no history. What it does is remove a
# per-machine setting from the decision: `core.autocrlf` is configured on the
# workstation rather than in the repository, so two developers could disagree
# about what a checkout looks like and neither would be wrong. That is an
# environment deciding a repository fact.
#
# `text=auto` lets git detect binary content itself rather than restating the
# extension list that tooling/verify/lib/util.mjs already owns as DECLARED_BINARY.
# One list, one home.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT CATCH, said plainly so it is not mistaken for a full fix:
# git normalises at checkout and at commit, not while a tool is writing. A
# generator or script that emits CRLF into the working tree still produces a
# stylesheet Biome rejects, and the failure surfaces at `format / lint` rather
# than here. That is exactly how it surfaced: a Python rewrite of a generator
# used the platform default newline and turned 856 lines CRLF.
* text=auto eol=lf
